WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
The Docker build context is the set of files available to the build.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:11.160
If a file enters the context, a Dockerfile can potentially copy it, inspect it, or be influenced by it.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:20.140
That makes build context hygiene part of image security, especially when repositories contain local configuration, generated files, or development-only assets.

4
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.220
.dockerignore helps exclude irrelevant or sensitive files from the build context.

5
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:42.520
Common exclusions include .git data, .env files, local secrets, certificates, private keys, logs, test output, local databases, IDE folders, temporary files, coverage output, and generated artifacts that should not ship.

6
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.440
A clean build context reduces accidental inclusion and can make builds faster and more reproducible.

7
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:55.220
It also makes review easier because the build only sees files that are plausible inputs to the image.

8
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.760
If every local artifact is available to the build, accidental copy patterns become harder to reason about.

9
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.240
Docker layers can preserve history and copied content.

10
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:13.780
Copying a sensitive file and deleting it in a later instruction is not a safe secret-handling strategy because earlier layers or build logs may still contain evidence of the file.

11
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:23.480
The safer approach is to keep sensitive files out of the build context and use appropriate temporary secret mechanisms when a build requires restricted access.

12
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.720
COPY and ADD should be narrow and intentional.

13
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:36.260
Prefer copying project files, published output, or specific directories rather than broad repository-level patterns that include everything.

14
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:44.760
ADD has extra behavior that is rarely needed for ordinary ASP.NET Core builds, so use it only when that behavior is intentional.

15
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.640
Before release, check the build output for unexpected files.

16
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:59.300
Look for source files, local configuration, test assets, development certificates, logs, package caches, temporary directories, and generated data.

17
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.680
Image contents should be deliberate, not whatever happened to be near the Dockerfile.
